!!<*yamlover: $defs: chapter>
What it doesn't test, it tolerates
description: consumers match by their own formula; unknown structure passes through
- >
  The spec mandates neither open nor closed matching. Every consumer — a renderer, a query, a
  validator — carries its **own acceptance formula** and claims a node exactly when the node satisfies
  it: *what it doesn't test, it tolerates*. A markdown renderer that only tests `format` keeps
  accepting a string that has grown annotation fields; a strict consumer may demand a closed shape;
  both are correct, each by its own formula (the [matching subchapter](::language:model:matching)).
- >
  The same tolerance holds across the language: mixtures of keyed and keyless entries, scalars with
  fields, unknown keys, unknown formats — all are ordinary data, never errors. Behavior is uniform
  over array, omni, mix, and scalar shapes; a consumer that cannot handle a shape is fixed, not the
  document.
